@@s001 Formal verification and JavaPathFinder
@@s002 ok:counter = 2 (right),no:counter = 1 (lost increment),ora:the counterexample found
@@s003 Two threads do <code>counter++</code> once each. Each increment is a read (<b>R</b>: temp = counter) and a write (<b>W</b>: counter = temp + 1). A model checker starts from the initial state…
@@s004 …and tries <b>all</b> the choices of the scheduler: at the first step t1 (R1) or t2 (R2) can run.
@@s005 Second step: for each branch, again all the possibilities.
@@s006 Third step.
@@s007 Fourth step: 6 interleavings in all. Only the 2 sequential ones (on the sides) give counter = <b>2</b>; in the other 4 both read 0 before writing, and counter is <b>1</b>.
@@s008 The counterexample to the property "at the end counter = 2": <b>R1, R2, W1, W2</b>. A test finds it only if it happens; the model checker always finds it, because it visits them all.
@@s009 dato:producer,due:consumer,no:deadlock,ok:solved
@@s010 dato:interleaving to explore,no:too many to visit
@@s011 Formal verification and JavaPathFinder
@@s012 Lecture 5, last part. Tests run the program and see only the interleavings that happen to occur. <b>Formal verification</b> treats the program and the property as mathematical objects and checks <b>all</b> the interleavings. JavaPathFinder (NASA) does it for Java programs with <b>model checking</b>.
@@s013 Exploring all the interleavings
@@s014 The tree of two counter++
@@s015 Tree built by hand; the results (2 correct final values out of 6) are confirmed by an exhaustive exploration in Python.
@@s016 The program of the slides: CounterAct.java, N = 10 increments per thread
@@s017 possible final values of counter: from <mark class="no">2</mark> to 20
@@s018 The "HARDer" question of the slides: the minimum is <b>2</b>, not 10. The interleaving: t1 reads 0; t2 does all its loops except the last; t1 writes 1; t2 reads 1; t1 finishes its loops; t2 writes 2. Build it yourself on the page <a href="l01-interleaving.html">Interleaving</a>.
@@s019 Trying them one by one is impossible: it is the <b>state explosion</b>.
@@s020 Many interleavings lead to the same state (same values of counter, temp and point in the code): a model checker such as JPF recognises them and does not explore them again (state matching). This is why the problem becomes tractable.
@@s021 assert (counter &gt; 1) → <mark class="ok">true in all executions</mark>
@@s022 With the minimum at 2, the assertion of <code>CounterAct</code> is never violated: JPF <b>proves</b> it true. With <code>counter &gt; 2</code> JPF would print the counterexample that reaches 2.
@@s023 Numbers computed with a small state explorer in Python, which does for this program the same thing as a model checker.
@@s024 Deadlock in the JPF bounded buffer
@@s025 The example <code>jpf_examples/deadlock/BoundedBuffer.java</code> uses a single monitor (<code>synchronized</code>, <code>wait()</code>) with <code>notify()</code> instead of <code>notifyAll()</code>. Producers and consumers wait in the same wait set, and <code>notify()</code> wakes <b>any one</b>. A scenario with a buffer of size 1, producers P1, P2 and consumers C1, C2:
@@s026 All stopped (scenario replayed step by step)
@@s027 The consumers arrive first.
@@s028 Waiting: [C2, P1, P2].
@@s029 The "wrong waiter": it wakes another consumer instead of a producer. Waiting: [P1, P2].
@@s030 Waiting: <b>[P1, P2, C1, C2]</b>. Nobody can notify anybody any more: <mark class="no">deadlock</mark>.
@@s031 with <mark class="ok">notifyAll()</mark> all wake up and each one rechecks its condition in the while
@@s032 A woken producer finds space and unblocks the situation. With tests a scenario like this happens rarely; JPF finds it by exploring the states (configuration <code>target.args = 2,4,1</code> in the .jpf file).
@@s033 notify() wakes the wrong waiter
@@s034 Why it is not always used
@@s035 <b>State explosion</b>: even small programs can have more states than can be visited. It is reduced with abstractions, approximations, and by restricting the problem.
@@s036 Tools: interactive provers (Rocq, Isabelle, Lean), model checkers, SAT/SMT solvers, static analysis. Example of the teacher's research: finding data races in large Java code bases with CodeQL (200 000 lines in less than 2 minutes).
@@s037 Industry uses it more and more (Intel for hardware; NASA used JPF for part of the Curiosity rover).
@@s038 At the exam
@@s039 Tests = generators of interleavings: they find counterexamples but do not prove the absence of errors. Formal verification = all the interleavings, with the cost of the state explosion. Being able to say what kind of property is being checked (<a href="l05-proprieta.html">safety or liveness</a>) and how its counterexample looks.
@@s040 State explosion
